# SYNOPSIS

```usage
rcport [-srdiIlxebpvV] [-c command] [-m rcmain] [file [arg ...]]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`rcport` is the library of our rc (`shell/rc/lib`) with a system layer for Linux, so that rc scripts can run on the dev machine.
Its only use is vx-rc-oracle(1), which runs each test case through it and through 9front's rc and compares them.
What it cannot show is the device's own system layer, which the boot tests check.

The arguments are rc's, and its flags are those of the usage line below.
The command runs the text of `-c` if given; else the script <file> with the <arg>s, which are `$*`; else the text read from standard input.
It exits 0 if rc's final status is empty (success) and 1 otherwise.
Like 9front's `rc` on Unix, it starts with:

- `path` as `. /bin /usr/bin /usr/local/bin` unless the environment has one, and `/bin` alone with `-p`;
- `home` from `HOME` when the environment has none;
- the environment's variables, a list's words separated by byte 1, as 9front's rc keeps them.

Streams are file descriptors and pipes are operating system pipes.
A program is looked up along `path` and started with `posix_spawn`.
An in-memory stream, such as a backquote's capture, is joined to a child through a pipe and a thread that pumps it.
A fork of rc, which on 9front is a process, is a thread here.
The signals `sigint`, `sigterm`, `sighup`, `sigquit`, `sigusr1`, `sigusr2`, `sigbus` and `sigwinch` arrive as the notes of those names, taken between commands; `SIGPIPE` is ignored.
At its end rc runs `sigexit`, unless it has exited already.

